    "original_title": "Pakistan oo duqeymo ka fuliyay Afghanistan, iyadoo Kabul ay sheegtay dhimashada Haween iyo Carruur",
    "slug": "pakistan-conducts-airstrikes-in-afghanistan-taliban-reports-civilian-casualties",
    "summary": "Pakistan launched overnight airstrikes in Afghanistan, saying it targeted militant hideouts and killed 22 fighters. Afghanistan reported at least 9 civilians killed, including women and children, in Kunar and Helmand provinces.",
    "content": "Pakistan carried out airstrikes inside Afghanistan on Wednesday night, according to statements from both governments. Islamabad said the strikes targeted hideouts and locations used by armed groups that it claims conduct attacks within Pakistan. The Pakistani military said 22 fighters were killed in the operation.\n\nThe Afghan government, however, reported that the strikes in Kunar and Helmand provinces resulted in at least 9 civilian deaths, including women and children, and left 11 others wounded. The differing accounts could not be independently verified.\n\nTensions between Pakistan and Afghanistan have risen in recent months over cross-border militancy. Pakistan has frequently accused the Afghan Taliban of providing sanctuary to groups such as Tehrik-i-Taliban Pakistan (TTP), a charge Kabul denies. Previous attempts at mediation, including by regional powers, have failed to produce a lasting agreement on border security.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this escalation adds to concerns about regional instability that can affect trade routes, humanitarian access, and security in the wider Muslim world. Many Somalis maintain business and family ties across the Middle East and South Asia, and any widening conflict could disrupt travel and remittances. The incident also underscores the challenges of militant movements operating across borders, a dynamic familiar in the Horn of Africa.\n\nInternational observers have called for restraint and dialogue to prevent further civilian harm. The United Nations has repeatedly urged both sides to protect non-combatants. As of now, no independent investigation has been announced.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/01/pakistan-oo-duqeymo-ka-fuliyay-afghanistan-iyadoo-kabul-ay-sheegtay-dhimashada-haween-iyo-carruur/",
