    "id": "news-1790856380107-15",
    "title": "Renewed Fighting in Northern Ethiopia Threatens 2022 Peace Deal",
    "original_title": "Wararkii ugu dambeeyay ee Dagaalka Itoobiya",
    "slug": "renewed-fighting-in-northern-ethiopia-threatens-2022-peace-deal",
    "summary": "Clashes between Ethiopian federal forces and Tigrayan groups have resumed in Tigray, spreading to Afar and Amhara, with at least 52 civilians killed in three days near Alamata.",
    "content": "Fighting has reignited in northern Ethiopia, with federal government forces and Tigrayan armed groups engaged in combat across various parts of the Tigray region. The conflict has also spread to the neighboring Afar and Amhara regions, according to reports. The renewed hostilities mark a significant escalation in a region that had seen relative calm since the signing of the Pretoria peace agreement in November 2022.\n\nThe Pretoria agreement had formally ended the two-year war that raged from 2020 to 2022, a conflict that resulted in massive loss of life and widespread displacement. That deal was hailed as a breakthrough, but its implementation has been fraught with challenges, including disputes over territorial control and the withdrawal of forces. The current clashes underscore the fragility of the peace process and the deep-seated tensions that remain unresolved.\n\nIn the latest violence, at least 52 civilians were reported killed over three days of fighting around the town of Alamata. The exact circumstances of their deaths remain unclear, but the toll highlights the severe impact on non-combatants. Local sources indicate that many residents have fled the area, exacerbating an already dire humanitarian situation.\n\nKey figures in the conflict include the Ethiopian federal government led by Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed and the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF), which governed Tigray before the war. The Pretoria agreement was mediated by the African Union, with former Nigerian President Olusegun Obasanjo as the AU's High Representative for the Horn of Africa. Both sides have accused each other of violating the ceasefire, though independent verification is difficult due to restricted access.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the renewed conflict in Ethiopia is of particular concern due to the country's proximity and its role as a major player in the Horn of Africa. Ethiopia hosts a large Somali refugee population and has been involved in peacekeeping efforts in Somalia. Instability in Ethiopia can spill over into Somalia, affecting trade routes, security, and regional stability. Many diaspora members have family and business ties across the border, making the situation a direct worry for their loved ones and investments.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/01/wararkii-ugu-dambeeyay-ee-dagaalka-itoobiya/",
